<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>spousalsupportcalc.com is written and reviewed by a small editorial team at The Click Lab. Spousal-support / alimony law is a state-by-state framework with substantial discretion baked in; getting the content right means working from primary sources (state statutes, state supreme court and appellate decisions, state judicial council forms, state bar guidance) rather than from secondhand summaries. We aim to produce content that an experienced family-law paralegal would consider technically accurate, and that a recently-separated reader without any legal background can still follow.</p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <p>Every guide on this Site follows the same workflow. A contributor drafts the article working from primary sources &mdash; the state's family code provisions, state-court guideline schedules, leading appellate opinions, and state judicial council forms or instructions. The editor-in-chief reviews the draft for accuracy of citation, internal consistency of analysis, and whether the article actually answers the question a real reader would ask. The calculator team verifies any formulas or worked examples against the state-specific schedule or the leading multi-factor analysis framework. Substantive updates (e.g., when a state revises its guideline) are dated; minor typographic corrections aren't.</p>
  <p>We don't publish "thin" content. Every guide is intended to answer a specific question a non-lawyer might ask &mdash; what's the formula in California, what counts as "income" for spousal-support purposes, what happens if the recipient cohabitates with a new partner, how is permanent alimony different from rehabilitative alimony &mdash; with enough specificity to be useful, while being honest about the limits of general informational content.</p>

  <h2>Our editorial team</h2>
  <p>Our editorial team works from primary family-law sources &mdash; state statutes, appellate decisions, and judicial-council guidelines &mdash; to keep the calculator and the state-by-state reference content accurate and current. No team member is a licensed attorney and none give legal advice; the team's role on this Site is editorial &mdash; reviewing every piece for technical accuracy against the underlying family-law sources and ensuring the calculator behaves consistently with the published state guidelines.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cite the authority.</strong> Every rule we discuss is grounded in a specific statute, appellate opinion, or state judicial-council guideline. When we describe a state's approach, we name the controlling statute and link to a publicly-available version where possible.</li>
    <li><strong>No legal advice.</strong> Everything on this site is general and informational. Specific spousal-support disputes need a licensed family-law attorney or qualified mediator. We don't review individual cases, don't predict outcomes, and don't represent anyone.</li>
    <li><strong>No paid placements in our editorial content.</strong> We don't accept money, gifts, or other consideration to recommend products, attorneys, law firms, mediators, or divorce-tech tools in our guides or methodology. The only ads on the Site are programmatic Google AdSense ads; we don't choose what runs. Separately, the case-review form on our homepage is a disclosed paid referral: submitting it shares your information with attorneys who may compensate The Click Lab Agency LLC for the introduction. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain language first.</strong> Family-law terms (<em>spousal support</em>, <em>alimony</em>, <em>maintenance</em>, <em>permanent alimony</em>, <em>rehabilitative alimony</em>, <em>durational alimony</em>, <em>cohabitation termination</em>, <em>imputed income</em>) are defined where they're first used. We assume the reader is sharp but not formally trained in family law.</li>
    <li><strong>Worked examples.</strong> Abstract statutory language without numbers and facts to anchor it is hard to apply. Most guides include at least one worked example with realistic income figures.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest about state variation.</strong> A single national summary of "how alimony works" is misleading. Where state law differs &mdash; and it differs a lot &mdash; we say so explicitly and direct the reader to the state-specific guide.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources we rely on</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>State family codes</strong> &mdash; the controlling statutory provisions in each state's domestic-relations or family code, particularly the alimony / spousal-support / maintenance sections.</li>
    <li><strong>State judicial council guidelines and forms</strong> &mdash; published spousal-support schedules, child-support guidelines (which interact with spousal-support computations), and standard family-law forms.</li>
    <li><strong>Appellate decisions</strong> &mdash; leading state supreme-court and appellate opinions interpreting spousal-support statutes, particularly on income definition, imputed income, modification standards, and termination triggers.</li>
    <li><strong>State bar family-law sections</strong> &mdash; published practitioner guides and CLE materials where freely accessible.</li>
    <li><strong>Federal tax-treatment background</strong> &mdash; the IRC changes effective with the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 (treatment of post-2018 alimony as non-taxable to recipient / non-deductible to payor for federal income tax) and their interaction with state-tax treatment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find an error &mdash; a wrong statutory citation, a state-law summary that's outdated, a calculator output that doesn't match the underlying formula &mdash; please email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> with the page URL and what's wrong. Verified corrections are typically made within a few business days. Material corrections are noted at the bottom of the affected page with the date of the change.</p>

  <h2>Reviewer disclosures</h2>
  <p>Our contributors are not licensed attorneys and do not practice law. Reviewing family-law content for accuracy is part of our editorial process and is not the same as giving legal advice. Nothing on this Site should be construed as legal advice, and nothing here creates an attorney-client relationship with any contributor or with The Click Lab.</p>

  <h2>Contact the editorial team</h2>
  <p>Editorial questions, citation suggestions, or interview requests: <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We respond within a few business days.</p>
</BaseLayout>
